NAME
       setfacl - set access control lists (ACLs) of files and directories

SYNOPSIS
       setfacl [-R] [-d] {-m | -x} ENTRIES FILE...
       setfacl [-R] {-b | -k} FILE...
       setfacl --set ENTRIES FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       ACLs give permissions to more people than the owner, the one group and
       everybody else: for example, read-write for the user alice and read
       for the group audit, on top of the normal mode bits. ls -l shows a +
       after the mode of a file that has an ACL; getfacl lists the entries.

       Entries are written as:

       u:NAME:PERMS  permissions for a user (u::PERMS is the owner)
       g:NAME:PERMS  permissions for a group (g::PERMS is the owning group)
       m::PERMS      the mask: the most any named user or group, and the
                     owning group, can get
       o::PERMS      everybody else

       PERMS is a combination of r, w and x (or X: execute only for
       directories and files that are already executable), or - for none.
       Several entries are separated by commas. With -x, give the entry
       without PERMS (u:alice).

       Default ACLs (-d, or entries starting with d:) belong to a directory
       and are copied to the files and directories created in it later.
       They do not change files that already exist; for those, run setfacl
       -R as well.

       setfacl recalculates the mask after each change, so it covers every
       named entry. A later chmod g=... on the file changes the mask, not the
       owning group's entry, and can take away rights the ACL gave.

       Only the owner of a file (or root) can change its ACL.

OPTIONS
       -m, --modify ENTRIES
              Add or change entries.

       -x, --remove ENTRIES
              Remove entries.

       -b, --remove-all
              Remove every ACL entry, leaving only the normal permissions.

       -k, --remove-default
              Remove the default ACL of a directory.

       -d, --default
              Apply the entries to the default ACL.

       -R, --recursive
              Apply to the directory and everything under it.

       -n, --no-mask
              Do not recalculate the mask.

       --mask Do recalculate the mask (the default).

       --set ENTRIES
              Replace the whole ACL. Must include u::, g:: and o:: entries.

       -M FILE, -X FILE
              Read the entries to add (-M) or remove (-X) from FILE.

       --test Show the result without changing anything.

EXAMPLES
       Let the user alice read and write /srv/report.txt, and the group
       audit only read it:

           setfacl -m u:alice:rw,g:audit:r /srv/report.txt
           getfacl /srv/report.txt

       Give student no access at all to /etc/fstab.copy:

           setfacl -m u:student:- /etc/fstab.copy

       Make every new file in /srv/team readable and writable by the group
       devs, and fix the files already there:

           setfacl -R -m g:devs:rwX /srv/team
           setfacl -m d:g:devs:rwX /srv/team

       Remove alice's entry, then every ACL entry:

           setfacl -x u:alice /srv/report.txt
           setfacl -b /srv/report.txt

NOTES
       cp -a, tar --acls and mv keep ACLs; plain cp does not. XFS and ext4
       support ACLs without extra mount options.

SEE ALSO
       getfacl(1), chmod(1), ls(1), umask(1), acl(5)
